<version>1.0.0</version>
<language>Rust</language>
Output src/philox.rs. std only, no unsafe. Philox4x32-10 counter-based generator (Salmon et al., SC'11, "Parallel random numbers: as easy as 1, 2, 3"), bit-identical to Random123's philox4x32 with 10 rounds: multipliers 0xD2511F53 and 0xCD9E8D57, Weyl key increments 0x9E3779B9 and 0xBB67AE85. pub fn philox4x32(counter:[u32;4], key:[u32;2])->[u32;4]. A stream type keyed by (seed:u64, stream identifiers) that a GPU kernel can reproduce exactly: struct Stream { key:[u32;2], counter:[u32;4], buffer, used } with Stream::new(seed:u64, a:u32, b:u32, c:u32) setting key = seed split into two u32 (low first), counter = [0, a, b, c]; next_u32 draws the four outputs of one block in order and then increments counter[0]; next_f64_open01 returns ((u64 from two u32, high first) >> 11) + 0.5) * 2^-53, in (0,1) never 0 or 1. Conventions for this crate: a = sweep, b = link index, c = replica. State the convention in the module doc so GPU code can follow it. Independent tests: tests/philox.rs checks the three Random123 known-answer vectors (zero counter and key; all-ones; the pi-digit vector) and basic uniformity (mean and variance of 10^6 draws within 6 standard errors).
